WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.125 --> 00:00:14.928
And now I'd like to present our speaker
today, Professor Julia Novy-Hildesley.

00:00:14.928 --> 00:00:17.820
Julia Novy-Hildesley is
Professor of the Practice and

00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:22.420
Executive Director of Stanford's Change
Leadership for Sustainability Program.

00:00:22.420 --> 00:00:24.050
This includes the Leadership for

00:00:24.050 --> 00:00:28.840
Sustainability Executive Education Program
as well as the Sustainability Science and

00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:31.880
Practice Interdisciplinary Master's
degree.

00:00:31.880 --> 00:00:36.785
With 20 years experience leading nonprofit
and philanthropic organizations, Julia's

00:00:36.785 --> 00:00:41.440
research and teaching focuses on business
strategies, leadership approaches and

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:44.825
cross-sector partnerships that
spur global development and

00:00:44.825 --> 00:00:48.685
align systems with the goal of
intergenerational well-being.

00:00:48.685 --> 00:00:52.835
The Change Leadership Program explores
the mindsets, knowledge and tools leaders

00:00:52.835 --> 00:00:57.900
needs to accelerate the transition to
a more sustainable and resilient society.

00:00:57.900 --> 00:01:00.658
These include understanding
complex systems,

00:01:00.658 --> 00:01:05.162
leading organizational change, and
innovating in complex systems at scale in

00:01:05.162 --> 00:01:09.546
order to shift suboptimal status quo
orientations toward sustainability.

00:01:09.546 --> 00:01:13.510
And Julia will be talking about some of
these themes today throughout the webinar.

00:01:13.510 --> 00:01:15.434
So with that I'd like to
turn it over to Julia.

00:01:19.341 --> 00:01:21.330
&gt;&gt; Hello there and welcome to webinar.

00:01:21.330 --> 00:01:22.650
Glad to have you joining us.

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:27.530
Today, I'm going to cover three
core questions and themes.

00:01:27.530 --> 00:01:30.020
First, why build
a sustainable organization?

00:01:30.020 --> 00:01:36.570
Second, what orientations are required to
thrive in today's operating environment?

00:01:36.570 --> 00:01:40.268
And, third, how to integrate
sustainability at the core and

00:01:40.268 --> 00:01:43.550
throughout your organization.

00:01:43.550 --> 00:01:47.747
So let's start first by talking
about sustainability and

00:01:47.747 --> 00:01:49.902
what sustainability means.

00:01:49.902 --> 00:01:55.796
Sustainability is really a term
that has often been associated

00:01:55.796 --> 00:02:00.970
with green concepts, and
doing environmental good.

00:02:00.970 --> 00:02:06.133
But, in fact, if you look at really
what sustainability means more broadly,

00:02:06.133 --> 00:02:11.310
it's a very holistic, systemic
definition that's important to consider.

00:02:11.310 --> 00:02:15.927
So, for example, economic,
social and environmental factors we

00:02:15.927 --> 00:02:19.735
hear when business talks
about the triple bottom line.

00:02:19.735 --> 00:02:24.013
The Brundtland Report is the first
report that really made an effort in

00:02:24.013 --> 00:02:27.362
1987 to define what
sustainable development is.

00:02:27.362 --> 00:02:30.662
And it looks at the interactions
between human beings and

00:02:30.662 --> 00:02:32.387
their natural environment.

00:02:32.387 --> 00:02:36.104
Basically saying that the environment
is the place where we live, and

00:02:36.104 --> 00:02:38.880
development is what we do
within that environment.

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:43.020
And therefore sustainable development is
thinking about how we go about improving

00:02:43.020 --> 00:02:47.710
our condition while maintaining the
quality of the environment where we live.

00:02:47.710 --> 00:02:51.740
Another really important thing to remember
about sustainability, in addition to it

00:02:51.740 --> 00:02:55.900
being a holistic term incorporating
social, economic and environmental values,

00:02:55.900 --> 00:03:01.630
is that it is a long-term
view on development.

00:03:01.630 --> 00:03:03.210
And it's a very integrated view.

00:03:03.210 --> 00:03:08.390
So, for example, it is not just about some
folks doing well at the expense of others.

00:03:09.450 --> 00:03:12.060
But it's really about a holistic
view of all people doing well.

00:03:12.060 --> 00:03:15.239
And it's about doing well
over time across generations.

00:03:16.340 --> 00:03:20.050
We often use the term intergenerational
well-being to describe what we mean

00:03:20.050 --> 00:03:20.870
by sustainability.

00:03:22.120 --> 00:03:25.290
So one can't really understand
sustainability without

00:03:25.290 --> 00:03:26.450
thinking about systems.

00:03:27.460 --> 00:03:31.680
Here in this diagram you see
the economy nested within society and

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:33.900
nested within the environment.

00:03:33.900 --> 00:03:37.992
When you think about systems,
you think about complex nonlinear dynamic

00:03:37.992 --> 00:03:42.160
interactions that occur between economy,
society and the environment.

00:03:42.160 --> 00:03:46.646
Jane Jacobs, an urban philosopher, reminds
us that the economy is a wholly owned

00:03:46.646 --> 00:03:50.102
subsidiary of the environment,
and not the other way around.

00:03:50.102 --> 00:03:55.523
So with this in mind, we have to move
a little bit from maybe our more linear,

00:03:55.523 --> 00:03:58.830
productive approaches
to problem solving and

00:03:58.830 --> 00:04:03.360
thinking, and pick a more holistic,
systemic view.

00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:06.612
This cartoon shows a group of
blind men with an elephant,

00:04:06.612 --> 00:04:09.475
working to understand
what they're looking at.

00:04:09.475 --> 00:04:13.145
And, of course, they're all looking
at different parts, separately, and

00:04:13.145 --> 00:04:15.270
getting fragmented views of what's going.

00:04:15.270 --> 00:04:19.595
It's like taking a systems perspective,
and exchanging information about different

00:04:19.595 --> 00:04:22.936
parts of the system, so that they
can come to a full understanding.

00:04:26.476 --> 00:04:32.547
Let's talk about how we hear in business
the global environment characterized.

00:04:32.547 --> 00:04:37.949
VUCA World is a concept that was coined
by the US Army War College to describe

00:04:37.949 --> 00:04:43.632
a multilateral world after the Cold War,
where things were much more in flux.

00:04:43.632 --> 00:04:48.177
And it has been used now since
the late 1990s in business to describe

00:04:48.177 --> 00:04:51.283
the volatility,
uncertainty, complexity and

00:04:51.283 --> 00:04:54.635
ambiguity of the business
operating environment.

00:04:54.635 --> 00:04:59.114
And, really, if you look at some of
the root causes of VUCA World, you see

00:04:59.114 --> 00:05:03.961
that underlying these systems are the
dynamic interactions between social and

00:05:03.961 --> 00:05:09.120
environment systems, and the increasing
stress we see that within those systems.

00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:15.035
Whether from population growth,
or technology change,

00:05:15.035 --> 00:05:19.380
or climate issues and resource scarcity,

00:05:19.380 --> 00:05:23.257
civic strife, or political unrest.

00:05:23.257 --> 00:05:27.912
These dynamics of the social
environmental systems are giving

00:05:27.912 --> 00:05:30.384
rise to the context we call VUCA.

00:05:30.384 --> 00:05:34.736
So given VUCA is characterized by
complexity, let's look a little bit more

00:05:34.736 --> 00:05:39.156
closely at what differentiates
complicated from complex environments, or

00:05:39.156 --> 00:05:42.160
complicated challenges
from complex challenges.

00:05:43.550 --> 00:05:48.920
Complicated challenges are ones where
the problem is easily definable,

00:05:48.920 --> 00:05:51.880
where existing know-how is adequate.

00:05:51.880 --> 00:05:56.443
You can solve the challenge
with an existing blueprint or

00:05:56.443 --> 00:05:58.540
algorithm or procedure.

00:05:58.540 --> 00:06:03.460
You're in a fairly stable context
where outcome is fairly predictable.

00:06:03.460 --> 00:06:06.151
You can plan for change.

00:06:06.151 --> 00:06:10.646
And success depends on the blueprint that
directs both the development of separate

00:06:10.646 --> 00:06:14.638
parts, and specifies the exact
relationship in which to assemble them.

00:06:14.638 --> 00:06:17.968
So you can think of a Ferrari
as a complicated system.

00:06:17.968 --> 00:06:20.271
You can take it all apart.

00:06:20.271 --> 00:06:23.641
And then while it's very, very complex,
we can put it back together.

00:06:23.641 --> 00:06:27.523
And the sum is simply equal to the parts.

00:06:27.523 --> 00:06:33.392
In a complex environment or challenge
only parts of the problem are definable.

00:06:33.392 --> 00:06:37.779
Expertise helps in the same way it
helps in a complicated challenge.

00:06:37.779 --> 00:06:41.598
But it has to be balanced with
responsiveness because the environment is

00:06:41.598 --> 00:06:42.230
changing.

00:06:44.200 --> 00:06:47.646
The context is unstable,
the outcome is unpredictable.

00:06:50.770 --> 00:06:54.343
And this, of course, means that
it requires constant adaptation,

00:06:54.343 --> 00:06:56.389
improvisation and experimentation.

00:06:56.389 --> 00:06:58.999
And in this case, you can't really
separate the parts from the whole.

00:06:58.999 --> 00:07:02.960
The essence actually exists in
the relationship between the parts.

00:07:02.960 --> 00:07:06.817
So you could think about a tropical
rainforest, for example, where you have

00:07:06.817 --> 00:07:10.750
all the different species together in
dynamic interaction with one another.

00:07:10.750 --> 00:07:15.690
And if you take it all apart, you will see
that actually the sum is much greater than

00:07:15.690 --> 00:07:18.782
the individual parts
because of the interaction.

00:07:18.782 --> 00:07:22.160
So the rainforest, for example,
produces rain on its own.

00:07:22.160 --> 00:07:23.770
It recycles nutrients.

00:07:23.770 --> 00:07:28.557
All of these functions happen
because of the interaction between

00:07:28.557 --> 00:07:30.218
the different parts.

00:07:30.218 --> 00:07:35.523
So here's a little cartoon that maybe
expresses our temptation to sometimes

00:07:35.523 --> 00:07:40.350
look for the simple answers when we
are overwhelmed in a VUCA context.

00:07:41.810 --> 00:07:46.647
That we are grappling with how
best to deal with complexity, and

00:07:46.647 --> 00:07:51.484
to find the patterns and
navigation that will help us find our way

00:07:51.484 --> 00:07:55.622
through complexity to
the visions we hope to achieve.

00:07:55.622 --> 00:08:00.795
David Snowden in the late 1990s worked
to try and help us in this regard.

00:08:00.795 --> 00:08:03.125
He developed the Cynefin framework.

00:08:03.125 --> 00:08:06.150
Cynefin is a word that
means habitat in Welsh.

00:08:06.150 --> 00:08:12.350
And you can see at the top left we have
environment characterized by complexity.

00:08:12.350 --> 00:08:16.000
So what this commitment framework does
is it characterizes different habitats

00:08:16.000 --> 00:08:17.470
of context.

00:08:17.470 --> 00:08:19.713
And the aim is to help
the policy makers and

00:08:19.713 --> 00:08:23.061
managers understand the different
context they might be in.

00:08:23.061 --> 00:08:27.121
And then apply the right
problem solving approaches or

00:08:27.121 --> 00:08:29.775
strategies based on that context.

00:08:29.775 --> 00:08:35.517
So if you look at the complex content
in the upper left quadrant, what you

00:08:35.517 --> 00:08:41.746
can see is that we need to apply intuitive
navigation from changing patterns.

00:08:41.746 --> 00:08:46.386
So we need to look at pattern management
as a dominant strategy when dealing with

00:08:46.386 --> 00:08:47.227
complexity.

00:08:47.227 --> 00:08:51.862
And that by definition means a really
strong capacity to sense the environments

00:08:51.862 --> 00:08:53.394
for perceived patterns.

00:08:53.394 --> 00:08:54.762
And then to respond and

00:08:54.762 --> 00:08:58.726
experiment accordingly based on
that strong sense in capacity.

00:08:58.726 --> 00:09:02.763
If we look at natural ecological systems,

00:09:02.763 --> 00:09:07.363
these systems like
the tropical rain forest have

00:09:07.363 --> 00:09:12.524
millions of year of experience
in navigating changing

00:09:12.524 --> 00:09:17.362
patterns in the context of complexity and
change.

00:09:17.362 --> 00:09:22.957
And an interesting question to consider
whether or not understanding a bit

00:09:22.957 --> 00:09:29.018
how the organisms wiithin the complex
open natural system like the rain forest.

00:09:29.018 --> 00:09:34.381
And the system itself orient could
perhaps help business and organizations

00:09:34.381 --> 00:09:39.669
think about how they might orient in
the context of change and complexity.

00:09:39.669 --> 00:09:44.172
So let's look at some of the core
orientations of resilient,

00:09:44.172 --> 00:09:46.085
open, natural systems.

00:09:46.085 --> 00:09:50.370
First of all they are oriented to connect.

00:09:50.370 --> 00:09:56.480
They have rich patterns of relationships
and very much of an interconnected,

00:09:56.480 --> 00:10:00.770
interdependent communication
between the different elements.

00:10:01.770 --> 00:10:06.224
They're also oriented to adapt, to
evolve and adapt to changing conditions.

00:10:06.224 --> 00:10:10.014
To be in touch with a sensing capacity
to connect things with what's going on

00:10:10.014 --> 00:10:11.092
in the environment.

00:10:11.092 --> 00:10:15.262
And then to adapt accordingly
to explore new niches or

00:10:15.262 --> 00:10:18.422
to evolve to suit a changing condition.

00:10:18.422 --> 00:10:22.623
And then finally they're
orients to innovate,

00:10:22.623 --> 00:10:28.259
to experiment and redesign,
to identify new ways of behaving and

00:10:28.259 --> 00:10:33.083
designing oneself in the context
of a new environment.

00:10:33.083 --> 00:10:40.051
So when you're thinking about building
a sustainable organization it's both

00:10:40.051 --> 00:10:45.052
about placing sustainability
at the core with purpose.

00:10:45.052 --> 00:10:48.665
But also applying the kinds
of orientation that really

00:10:48.665 --> 00:10:53.624
enable an organization to thrive in
the context of complexity and change.

00:10:53.624 --> 00:10:58.000
So you can see in this diagram the idea
that applying orientation of connect,

00:10:58.000 --> 00:10:59.223
adapt and innovate.

00:10:59.223 --> 00:11:03.476
Expands the capacity of the organization
because it has impact through its core

00:11:03.476 --> 00:11:04.094
purpose.

00:11:04.094 --> 00:11:09.284
So let's talk a little bit
about what these orientations

00:11:09.284 --> 00:11:13.294
might look like in
the context of business.

00:11:13.294 --> 00:11:19.347
So connect, for example would be about
broadening and deepening connections.

00:11:19.347 --> 00:11:21.242
So broadening one's views,

00:11:21.242 --> 00:11:26.261
seeing one's connection to the broader
context and operating environment.

00:11:26.261 --> 00:11:28.728
And then, deepening the connection say,
for example through one's value chain.

00:11:28.728 --> 00:11:33.031
You have more of a relationship focus
rather than a transaction focus to really

00:11:33.031 --> 00:11:38.290
we understand the operations more deeply
and build the relationships accordingly.

00:11:38.290 --> 00:11:41.130
It's about seeing the environment in

00:11:41.130 --> 00:11:43.136
which you're operating from
a holistic perspective.

00:11:43.136 --> 00:11:47.183
And that includes seeing all
the connections as well as seeing places

00:11:47.183 --> 00:11:51.244
maybe where there are disconnections or
where the system is broken.

00:11:51.244 --> 00:11:55.597
Adapt, is really about learning and
reorienting to be relevant,

00:11:55.597 --> 00:11:58.330
being relevant to the changing context.

00:11:58.330 --> 00:12:03.207
That means seeing the fuller
understanding of reality that you get

00:12:03.207 --> 00:12:05.043
by greater connection.

00:12:05.043 --> 00:12:09.155
And then expanding a notion of
responsibility because of that

00:12:09.155 --> 00:12:10.347
understanding.

00:12:10.347 --> 00:12:14.666
So that may mean moving from focus
on shareholders as the dominant and

00:12:14.666 --> 00:12:20.010
only stakeholders to a much more broad and
inclusive view of stakeholders.

00:12:20.010 --> 00:12:25.590
It may mean moving from the shorter term
focus on financial profitability to a more

00:12:25.590 --> 00:12:31.790
inclusive longer term focus on social,
environmental and economic sustainability.

00:12:31.790 --> 00:12:36.310
And then in terms of innovate it's
really about thinking creatively

00:12:36.310 --> 00:12:40.180
about the opportunities presented
by the new context and designing.

00:12:40.180 --> 00:12:42.650
And partnering for sustainability.

00:12:42.650 --> 00:12:47.626
Taking some responsibility for
the ways in which the system

00:12:47.626 --> 00:12:52.417
isn't working and jumping in and
redesigning systems.

00:12:52.417 --> 00:12:57.126
So that they can work in this new context
and align with sustainability goals.

00:12:57.126 --> 00:13:01.038
So in a moment we're going to talk
about a couple of organizations.

00:13:01.038 --> 00:13:06.422
Look at two examples of organizations
that have put sustainability at the core.

00:13:06.422 --> 00:13:10.260
And applied these orientations
to expand the impact and

00:13:10.260 --> 00:13:13.043
profitability of their organization.

00:13:13.043 --> 00:13:17.867
But first I'd like to do a quick poll and
check in with our group here about some of

00:13:17.867 --> 00:13:21.115
the motivations that might
be behind your interest.

00:13:21.115 --> 00:13:24.300
And your organization's interest
in engaging in sustainability.

00:13:24.300 --> 00:13:28.281
So what I'd like to ask you or
what are the main driver for

00:13:28.281 --> 00:13:33.899
integrating sustainability and into
the core purpose of your organization?

00:13:33.899 --> 00:13:38.550
There's a set of options
there to choose from.

00:13:38.550 --> 00:13:43.580
These are based on the report
the comprehensive report submitted

00:13:43.580 --> 00:13:46.680
to the Harvard Business Reviews
on public last

00:13:46.680 --> 00:13:50.090
that really looked at
the academic research.

00:13:50.090 --> 00:13:53.945
And data that has been, for
the last couple of decades,

00:13:53.945 --> 00:13:58.140
as to why companies are putting
sustainability at the core.

00:13:58.140 --> 00:14:02.642
I'd be interested to hear what's
motivating your organization to do so

00:14:02.642 --> 00:14:04.202
to the extent that it is.

00:14:04.202 --> 00:14:06.270
So we'll pause for a moment.

00:14:06.270 --> 00:14:12.612
Take some answers and then in a minute or
two be able to report back on the poll.

00:14:12.612 --> 00:14:14.046
Okay, we'll close the poll.

00:14:14.046 --> 00:14:18.778
It looks though fostering innovation
is one of the leading reasons.

00:14:18.778 --> 00:14:23.529
And driving competitive advantage as well
as the tracking and engaging of employees,

00:14:23.529 --> 00:14:26.663
that looks like that also
received quite a large number.

00:14:26.663 --> 00:14:29.876
But all of the choices were selected.

00:14:29.876 --> 00:14:32.023
Okay, great, thank you.

00:14:32.023 --> 00:14:35.248
So let's look at a couple of case studies.

00:14:35.248 --> 00:14:39.805
Unilever, a multinational consumer
goods company which reaches 2 billion

00:14:39.805 --> 00:14:44.010
customers per day and
has over 170,000 employees.

00:14:44.010 --> 00:14:48.584
And Sustainable Harvest, a specialty
coffee importer that imports about 20

00:14:48.584 --> 00:14:51.330
million pounds of coffee per year.

00:14:51.330 --> 00:14:52.877
And it's responsible for

00:14:52.877 --> 00:14:56.961
about one-sixth of the fair trade
coffee sold in the United States.

00:14:56.961 --> 00:15:01.832
So let's look at the orientation that
they're applying with sustainability

00:15:01.832 --> 00:15:03.381
as their core purpose and

00:15:03.381 --> 00:15:07.378
see what that actually means if
you translate that to business.

00:15:07.378 --> 00:15:09.544
So first of all the connect orientation.

00:15:09.544 --> 00:15:14.088
They are broadening their view,
looking at the broader context and

00:15:14.088 --> 00:15:16.810
also deepening connections.

00:15:16.810 --> 00:15:19.994
And they're doing this in three ways
that I'd like to discuss today.

00:15:19.994 --> 00:15:23.859
One, their situating their organization
in the broader context, understanding

00:15:23.859 --> 00:15:27.297
the role they play in that context and
how that context might affect them.

00:15:27.297 --> 00:15:31.731
Second, they're building relationships
across their value chain and

00:15:31.731 --> 00:15:35.887
third their connecting their core
purpose to the current reality.

00:15:35.887 --> 00:15:40.484
So they're showing an alignment
between their core purpose and

00:15:40.484 --> 00:15:43.132
the context they're operating in.

00:15:43.132 --> 00:15:44.623
Second, the adapt orientation.

00:15:44.623 --> 00:15:47.943
This is really about learning and
reorienting to be relevant.

00:15:47.943 --> 00:15:53.053
So in the first place they're
reorienting goals and strategy to align

00:15:53.053 --> 00:15:58.257
with the new sustainability orientation
and the contextual changes.

00:15:58.257 --> 00:16:01.746
But they're also putting in place
metrics to measure progress.

00:16:01.746 --> 00:16:04.715
Second they're aligning culture and
incentives.

00:16:04.715 --> 00:16:06.110
The.

00:16:06.110 --> 00:16:09.860
Phrase that culture often trumps
strategy is important here in this.

00:16:09.860 --> 00:16:12.810
Without a culture that's
supportive of this realignment and

00:16:12.810 --> 00:16:17.550
its learning, sustainability and
strategy will likely not be as effective.

00:16:18.560 --> 00:16:23.412
And then finally, adopting a learning
orientation throughout the organization.

00:16:23.412 --> 00:16:25.495
Third, innovating.

00:16:25.495 --> 00:16:29.089
You all selected fostering innovation
as one of the core reasons for

00:16:29.089 --> 00:16:33.230
putting sustainability at
the core of your organization.

00:16:33.230 --> 00:16:37.170
This really is about designing and
partnering for sustainability.

00:16:37.170 --> 00:16:40.593
First, through new products and
business models.

00:16:40.593 --> 00:16:44.805
Second, by engaging broad
participation in problem solving.

00:16:44.805 --> 00:16:48.592
So, harnessing a lot more resources
dedicated to that innovation.

00:16:48.592 --> 00:16:51.590
And then finally, forging partnerships,
new kinds of partnerships.

00:16:51.590 --> 00:16:55.350
Perhaps with unlikely bedfellows
to achieve scaled impact.

00:16:57.530 --> 00:16:59.780
So, let's dig into Unilever a little bit.

00:16:59.780 --> 00:17:00.620
As you probably know,

00:17:00.620 --> 00:17:04.358
Unilever is an Anglo-Dutch
transnational consumer goods company.

00:17:04.358 --> 00:17:08.010
It touches 2 billion customers per day.

00:17:08.010 --> 00:17:13.080
And the CEO, Paul Polman, began his tenure

00:17:13.080 --> 00:17:16.900
in 2009 and built on
the traditional values of Unilever,

00:17:16.900 --> 00:17:21.865
but really began a very powerful and
focused emphasis on sustainability.

00:17:21.865 --> 00:17:26.588
Focusing on growing and prospering
by tackling 21st century challenges.

00:17:26.588 --> 00:17:32.830
Actually seeing as we pointed out in the
pool that by tackling these challenges for

00:17:32.830 --> 00:17:37.816
innovation and therefore
productivity growth would increase.

00:17:37.816 --> 00:17:42.390
So, one of the first things we talked
about was taking this systems approach.

00:17:42.390 --> 00:17:46.785
He said business needs to take more of
a systems approach to their operations,

00:17:46.785 --> 00:17:50.716
seeing companies as part of
an interconnected web that links multiple

00:17:50.716 --> 00:17:52.260
players.

00:17:52.260 --> 00:17:55.227
So, this is how he began his approach and

00:17:55.227 --> 00:18:00.567
foray to thinking about operating
business services sustainability.

00:18:00.567 --> 00:18:05.253
So, the first element of situating
in broader context aligned with

00:18:05.253 --> 00:18:07.820
the connect orientation.

00:18:07.820 --> 00:18:11.678
Unilever conducted a thorough
strategic review and

00:18:11.678 --> 00:18:15.981
identified four megatrends
that affected its business.

00:18:15.981 --> 00:18:20.494
The first has to do with shift in markets,
the world moving south and east,

00:18:20.494 --> 00:18:24.540
in other words a strong
shift to emerging markets.

00:18:24.540 --> 00:18:28.924
But then, recognizing that within these
markets, there was some fringe related

00:18:28.924 --> 00:18:32.879
to income and resource scarcity that
would have impacts on the business.

00:18:32.879 --> 00:18:36.712
Second, shift in lifestyles,
living differently.

00:18:36.712 --> 00:18:42.666
A big move of globally from rural
to urban locations which then put

00:18:42.666 --> 00:18:51.050
into question taxing already strained
infrastructure with this urban migration.

00:18:51.050 --> 00:18:52.960
Also longer lives, people living longer.

00:18:52.960 --> 00:18:59.843
All of these had implications for supply
chains and products and service designs.

00:18:59.843 --> 00:19:04.936
Third, the environment under stress,
shift in the natural environment.

00:19:04.936 --> 00:19:10.012
Recognizing water scarcity, climate
change, deforestation, poor sanitation

00:19:10.012 --> 00:19:14.807
that the operating conditions for
business were increasingly under stress.

00:19:14.807 --> 00:19:19.451
Which had big importation for
product development and manufacturing,

00:19:19.451 --> 00:19:21.819
as well as pricing security issues.

00:19:21.819 --> 00:19:24.930
And then finally, the digital revolution.

00:19:24.930 --> 00:19:28.660
Looking at shift and
stakeholder empowerment through access to

00:19:28.660 --> 00:19:31.190
information and
communication technologies.

00:19:31.190 --> 00:19:35.581
In particular, recognizing that
citizens who were empowered to these

00:19:35.581 --> 00:19:40.129
technologies required more transparency
and stakeholder engagement.

00:19:40.129 --> 00:19:45.095
And that was required in order to
minimize the reputational risks and

00:19:45.095 --> 00:19:48.880
the demands for
more stringent regulations.

00:19:48.880 --> 00:19:55.685
The business might suffer if it didn't
effectively engaged the stakeholders So,

00:19:55.685 --> 00:20:00.712
in addition to connecting with
the broader context, Unilever also has

00:20:00.712 --> 00:20:05.920
placed a very strong emphasis on
connecting throughout the value chain.

00:20:05.920 --> 00:20:10.507
So, not just the broader global
environment, but then really looking

00:20:10.507 --> 00:20:15.094
across all of the value chains and
thinking about the relationship with

00:20:15.094 --> 00:20:19.237
supplier and the impact of
the product within that value chain.

00:20:19.237 --> 00:20:24.685
So, one step comment took early on was to
ask if managers to work across discipline,

00:20:24.685 --> 00:20:29.720
to deepen relationship and
understanding across the value chain.

00:20:29.720 --> 00:20:33.883
Working with producers and suppliers to
understand the environmental impact and

00:20:33.883 --> 00:20:36.250
social impact of its product.

00:20:36.250 --> 00:20:43.749
So, it conducted a life cycle analysis
of the company's top 13 brands.

00:20:43.749 --> 00:20:48.820
And as you may know, a life cycle analysis
is technique that we used to thoroughly

00:20:48.820 --> 00:20:54.054
assess the environmental impact associated
with all stages of a product's life.

00:20:54.054 --> 00:20:58.983
From raw material extraction
through materials processing,

00:20:58.983 --> 00:21:04.010
manufacture, distribution,
use, repair and maintenance.

00:21:08.800 --> 00:21:13.778
So, through this connection
with the broader context,

00:21:13.778 --> 00:21:16.795
connection with the value chain.

00:21:16.795 --> 00:21:19.281
Unilever really is connected
with its core purpose and

00:21:19.281 --> 00:21:23.970
built on founding values since the 1880s,
which were around responsible business.

00:21:23.970 --> 00:21:27.032
Business developing products that
met the needs of the masses.

00:21:27.032 --> 00:21:31.122
And we formulated that core purpose
to focus on what they thought was

00:21:31.122 --> 00:21:34.250
to call the Unilever in the 21st century.

00:21:34.250 --> 00:21:37.996
And that was to make
the sustainable living commonplace.

00:21:37.996 --> 00:21:41.220
So, and it has adopted some of the,

00:21:41.220 --> 00:21:45.665
One of the great system scientist
from the 20th century and

00:21:45.665 --> 00:21:51.530
she talks about the tension of creating
what is in the staff to create a purpose.

00:21:51.530 --> 00:21:54.662
In this case, making sustainable living,
common price, and

00:21:54.662 --> 00:21:56.975
really understanding your current reality.

00:21:56.975 --> 00:22:02.053
And the importance of really holding
both of those to create attention that

00:22:02.053 --> 00:22:08.107
forces realignment and change and progress
hopefully towards achieving your purpose.

00:22:08.107 --> 00:22:12.834
And this is where they adapt,
orientation comes in where businesses,

00:22:12.834 --> 00:22:16.534
they need to think about,
how did they need to change and

00:22:16.534 --> 00:22:21.450
reorient to achieve this core purpose
in the context of current reality?

00:22:23.080 --> 00:22:26.190
Paul Polman came out and said,
we must develop a business model aimed at

00:22:26.190 --> 00:22:30.080
contributing to society and the
environment instead of taking from them.

00:22:30.080 --> 00:22:33.425
Somehow, you need to figure out what
the fitness model can look like and

00:22:33.425 --> 00:22:34.500
we'll achieve that.

00:22:34.500 --> 00:22:38.581
And so, they developed
a business model that situated

00:22:38.581 --> 00:22:41.920
except around sustainable living.

00:22:41.920 --> 00:22:47.443
Basically the idea was that profitable
volume growth would occur through

00:22:47.443 --> 00:22:53.693
innovations that bring new sustainability
benefits to consumers and retailers.

00:22:53.693 --> 00:22:58.369
That reducing waste and energy by
thinking about sustainability and

00:22:58.369 --> 00:23:02.408
looking at looking at life
cycle analysis would save cost.

00:23:02.408 --> 00:23:07.549
And that the risks to be managed in the
supply chain, which would secure long-term

00:23:07.549 --> 00:23:12.410
sustainable sourcing of materials and
long-term viability of operations.

00:23:15.260 --> 00:23:18.750
So, through this business plan,
it set out a vision and

00:23:18.750 --> 00:23:23.050
a series of goals called
the sustainable living plan.

00:23:23.050 --> 00:23:28.230
It was a vision to double the size
of their business while reducing its

00:23:28.230 --> 00:23:34.090
environmental footprint and increasing the
positive social impact of the business.

00:23:34.090 --> 00:23:38.700
This was set out in 2010 as
a ten-year vision for profitable and

00:23:38.700 --> 00:23:39.470
sustainable growth.

00:23:40.750 --> 00:23:45.839
Within that plan,
three concrete goals were identified.

00:23:45.839 --> 00:23:50.300
It can help more than 1 billion people
to improve their health and well-being.

00:23:50.300 --> 00:23:53.440
By 2020 to halve the environment
footprint of Unilever products.

00:23:54.940 --> 00:23:59.480
And to source 100% of agricultural
raw materials sustainability and

00:23:59.480 --> 00:24:03.110
enhance the livelihoods of millions
of people around the value chain.

00:24:03.110 --> 00:24:06.020
Again, this is back to that expansion,
not just of connection but

00:24:06.020 --> 00:24:09.870
a sense of responsibility
throughout the value chain.

00:24:11.700 --> 00:24:15.580
Within the Sustainable Living Plan,
there are nine goals.

00:24:15.580 --> 00:24:19.082
They center around three areas,
improving health and well-being,

00:24:19.082 --> 00:24:22.120
reducing environmental impact and
enhancing livelihood.

00:24:24.418 --> 00:24:29.530
Of course, goals need metrics to ensure
there's progress and Unilever brings

00:24:29.530 --> 00:24:34.780
very strong on establishing over 50
targets associated with the goals and

00:24:34.780 --> 00:24:39.220
then tracking progress and
reporting progress against their targets.

00:24:39.220 --> 00:24:44.070
In this case, this is a 2014 Sustainable
Living Plan summary of progress and

00:24:44.070 --> 00:24:47.690
one example, for example,
is the health and hygiene,

00:24:47.690 --> 00:24:53.480
the goal was to help 1 billion
people improve hygiene habits.

00:24:53.480 --> 00:24:58.540
By 2014, Unilever had reached 397
million people through sanitation,

00:24:58.540 --> 00:25:01.210
safe drinking water, and
hand washing campaigns.

00:25:03.840 --> 00:25:08.840
It has also have been reporting financial
and non-financial indicators next to

00:25:08.840 --> 00:25:13.399
one another, to also cultivate that
awareness among all stakeholders,

00:25:13.399 --> 00:25:19.050
the board all the way down to consumers,
that it is tracking both of these things.

00:25:19.050 --> 00:25:22.208
Here you can see at the top, the more
traditional financial indicators and

00:25:22.208 --> 00:25:26.300
below, non-financial indicators
around manufacturing.

00:25:26.300 --> 00:25:30.440
For example,
looking at carbon generated and

00:25:30.440 --> 00:25:34.833
water use for counter production,
waste and disposal and also,

00:25:34.833 --> 00:25:40.940
issues around employee engagement and
diversity that are being tracked.

00:25:43.450 --> 00:25:48.230
All this is happening in the context of
Unilever's share price doing quite well,

00:25:48.230 --> 00:25:50.060
the two red lines there
on those two graphs,

00:25:50.060 --> 00:25:54.835
compared to the S&amp;P 500 on the left and
the FTSE 100 on the right.

00:25:57.135 --> 00:26:02.224
Unilever continues to show
positive financial indicators and

00:26:02.224 --> 00:26:08.369
in 2014, 50% of its growth came
from the Sustainable Living brand,

00:26:08.369 --> 00:26:12.800
they grew at twice the rate
of the rest of the business.

00:26:19.017 --> 00:26:23.473
In addition to aligning goals and
metrics with sustainability,

00:26:23.473 --> 00:26:29.144
as we discussed earlier, aligning cultural
incentives is critical, so Unilever

00:26:29.144 --> 00:26:35.110
has pursued a full integration of the
Sustainable Living Plan into its culture.

00:26:35.110 --> 00:26:40.070
There are two board committees entirely
responsible for evaluating progress,

00:26:40.070 --> 00:26:44.440
making recommendations, critiquing, issues
related to the Sustainable Living Plan.

00:26:44.440 --> 00:26:49.680
Paul Polman early on decided
not to issue quarterly reports.

00:26:49.680 --> 00:26:54.502
He wanted to underscore management
belief that investing in sustainability

00:26:54.502 --> 00:26:59.250
takes time, and remuneration is tied
to achieving targets associated with

00:26:59.250 --> 00:27:01.190
the Sustainable Living Plan.

00:27:01.190 --> 00:27:07.093
In addition to aligning
the incentives of the culture,

00:27:07.093 --> 00:27:14.960
one core aspect of culture change is
developing a learning orientation.

00:27:14.960 --> 00:27:18.870
How could Unilever develop
a learning organization so

00:27:18.870 --> 00:27:22.490
that it would not just reorient once,
but continue to reorient and

00:27:22.490 --> 00:27:26.947
adapt as conditions change and through
a better understanding of its environment?

00:27:26.947 --> 00:27:34.360
It embarked on quite extensive employee
training and development endeavors.

00:27:34.360 --> 00:27:40.110
Both communicating the core purpose of the
institution as well as trying to cultivate

00:27:40.110 --> 00:27:45.110
both mindset and other system thinking
approaches amongst its employees.

00:27:47.290 --> 00:27:51.800
It's also really pursued
the idea that involving

00:27:51.800 --> 00:27:55.650
employees is central to
helping them understand and

00:27:55.650 --> 00:28:01.200
feel committed and engaged with
the purpose of the organization.

00:28:01.200 --> 00:28:06.730
A couple of examples of how it's done that
include the Dove self-esteem project,

00:28:06.730 --> 00:28:11.360
where Dove Soap has been associated
with the opportunity to educate girls.

00:28:12.380 --> 00:28:16.090
Between the ages of eight and
17 on the beauty comes from within and

00:28:16.090 --> 00:28:21.130
help combat some of the issues that young
women are facing in terms of body image.

00:28:21.130 --> 00:28:24.030
Employees have been very involved
in rolling out those campaigns in

00:28:24.030 --> 00:28:25.780
diverse countries.

00:28:25.780 --> 00:28:30.680
Similarly, Help a Child Reach Five
Campaign is a campaign that has

00:28:30.680 --> 00:28:34.980
been teaching children and their parents
about the importance of handwashing and

00:28:34.980 --> 00:28:36.040
how to do it effectively.

00:28:37.120 --> 00:28:40.070
Employees have been very engaged
in participating in those training

00:28:40.070 --> 00:28:40.970
programs, as well.

00:28:42.940 --> 00:28:48.530
Learning from stakeholders is another key
aspect of adopting learning orientations.

00:28:48.530 --> 00:28:51.120
Not just building your own capacity
within the organization to learn,

00:28:51.120 --> 00:28:55.240
but thinking about how you learn
from diverse stakeholders,

00:28:55.240 --> 00:29:00.676
bringing as many ideas to the table for
innovation.

00:29:00.676 --> 00:29:07.390
One example is Unilever established
the Sustainable Living Lab

00:29:07.390 --> 00:29:12.220
which was a scaled approach to stakeholder
engagement where it engaged over 2,000

00:29:12.220 --> 00:29:16.430
stakeholders from 70 countries
in a 24 hour live dialogue.

00:29:16.430 --> 00:29:22.520
To critique and reflect on progress within
the Sustainable Living Plan thus far, and

00:29:22.520 --> 00:29:29.060
to gain new ideas for
how to make it better progress.

00:29:29.060 --> 00:29:31.330
Moving to an innovate orientation.

00:29:33.640 --> 00:29:39.380
Once the organization is reoriented and
is aligning culture and learning

00:29:39.380 --> 00:29:44.920
with sustainability, how can it then
move to a platform of innovation?

00:29:44.920 --> 00:29:48.030
One of the things that Unilever has put
a lot of emphasis on is designing new

00:29:48.030 --> 00:29:51.250
products and business models that
tackle sustainability challenges.

00:29:52.550 --> 00:29:54.950
The Sunlight and
Lifebuoy soaps are two great examples.

00:29:54.950 --> 00:29:56.803
Lifebuoy soap, in particular,

00:29:56.803 --> 00:30:00.250
is one you can see the children's
hands turning green there.

00:30:00.250 --> 00:30:04.850
They designed this soap so that it would
turn green once children had washed

00:30:04.850 --> 00:30:08.400
their hands long enough to
eliminate 99.9% of germs.

00:30:08.400 --> 00:30:12.430
They also used a redesigned formulation so
that it was a foam wash so

00:30:12.430 --> 00:30:15.140
it was more relevant to
places with water scarcity,

00:30:15.140 --> 00:30:19.530
it requires a third less water,
because it's a foam soap.

00:30:21.030 --> 00:30:25.740
Finally, by doing research on
the germs that cause cholera and

00:30:25.740 --> 00:30:28.530
typhoid and eye and
skin infections, the most

00:30:28.530 --> 00:30:32.296
significant causes of infant mortality in
a number of the regions where they work.

00:30:32.296 --> 00:30:37.174
They reformulated the actual chemical
composition of the soap to be ten

00:30:37.174 --> 00:30:41.910
times more effective at killing
those particular germs.

00:30:41.910 --> 00:30:45.520
As you saw on the prior slide,
launching campaigns to really help

00:30:45.520 --> 00:30:50.040
educate consumers, both in terms of issues
of water scarcity, but also in terms of

00:30:50.040 --> 00:30:55.280
the benefits of handwashing for
sustainability and livelihood.

00:30:57.290 --> 00:31:01.868
Circular Economy is another area
where Unilever has innovated.

00:31:01.868 --> 00:31:07.460
They are taking the traditional
model of take makes waste,

00:31:07.460 --> 00:31:11.970
which is take resources from
the natural environment,

00:31:11.970 --> 00:31:16.050
make products, use them for a limited
period of time and then dispose of them,

00:31:16.050 --> 00:31:17.820
to thinking much more in a circular way.

00:31:17.820 --> 00:31:23.440
In the same way that natural systems
pursue a circular approach to waste,

00:31:23.440 --> 00:31:26.270
where all waste is recycled and
re-used by the system.

00:31:27.310 --> 00:31:30.720
Unilever has incentivized
in groups throughout its

00:31:30.720 --> 00:31:35.540
supply chain to think about
Circular Economy opportunities.

00:31:35.540 --> 00:31:43.870
Some ideas include using tea bags for
wallpaper, they've gotten plastic

00:31:43.870 --> 00:31:48.300
laminate that used to go into landfills
to be now used for school desks.

00:31:48.300 --> 00:31:50.410
Mayonnaise turning to biofuel, so

00:31:50.410 --> 00:31:55.480
a lot of creativity happening
in their journey to zero waste.

00:31:57.910 --> 00:32:00.750
The other thing they're doing
is designing and partnering for

00:32:00.750 --> 00:32:05.490
sustainability by engaging broad
participation in problem-solving and

00:32:05.490 --> 00:32:09.200
employee incentive programs that
they use to fund projects globally

00:32:10.220 --> 00:32:13.240
that are having a significant
impact on carbon reduction.

00:32:13.240 --> 00:32:17.240
They are also crowdsourcing
sustainability through the innovation,

00:32:17.240 --> 00:32:20.830
Unilever Foundry is a open
innovation platform.

00:32:20.830 --> 00:32:24.109
And Unilever post challenges and

00:32:24.109 --> 00:32:29.739
opens it up to global participation and
collaboration.

00:32:29.739 --> 00:32:35.417
Finally, Unilever has been a leader
in terms of working with unlikely and

00:32:35.417 --> 00:32:38.070
partners for sustainability.

00:32:38.070 --> 00:32:42.360
Back in the late 1990s, Unilever formed
a partnership with World Wildlife fund,

00:32:42.360 --> 00:32:46.670
a global conservation organization to
launch them into a Stewardship Council,

00:32:46.670 --> 00:32:47.575
a certification and

00:32:47.575 --> 00:32:52.510
eco-labeling organization that would
help create economic incentives for

00:32:52.510 --> 00:32:56.400
sustainable fishing by certifying
those fisheries with good practice and

00:32:56.400 --> 00:33:00.990
align products from those
fisheries to bear an eco-label.

00:33:00.990 --> 00:33:05.220
It also is more recently working with
the Tropical Forest Alliance, a group of

00:33:05.220 --> 00:33:09.920
government and consumer good companies
who are dedicating to eliminating

00:33:09.920 --> 00:33:13.680
deforestation entirely from the supply
chain of consumer goods companies and

00:33:13.680 --> 00:33:17.190
figuring out strategies to
address that challenge.

00:33:19.060 --> 00:33:21.460
So let's turn briefly
to sustainable harvest.

00:33:21.460 --> 00:33:26.410
A second company, a specialty coffee
importer that sells certified

00:33:26.410 --> 00:33:30.940
specialty coffee from about 18 countries,
approximately 20 million pounds

00:33:30.940 --> 00:33:35.300
imported each year, and as I mentioned
a sixth of certified US fair trade coffee.

00:33:37.190 --> 00:33:42.680
Sustainable harvest started in the late
1980s when its founder Dave Griswold

00:33:42.680 --> 00:33:48.770
was volunteering in Mexico for a coffee
organization that supported farmers and

00:33:48.770 --> 00:33:53.770
met Pedro, a farmer who came and
represented 40 families and

00:33:53.770 --> 00:33:57.900
handed Dave a bag of coffee and
said, this is really good coffee.

00:33:57.900 --> 00:34:00.944
I come from the state in Mexico and

00:34:00.944 --> 00:34:05.234
we need help finding ways to sell
our coffee, can you help us?

00:34:05.234 --> 00:34:09.624
And, so what they did, if you thought
about what this meant, the fact that there

00:34:09.624 --> 00:34:14.018
was such a disconnect in the global market
where farmers were producing coffee,

00:34:14.018 --> 00:34:16.925
that they had no idea how to
access the global market,

00:34:16.925 --> 00:34:19.360
they were just selling on local exchanges.

00:34:19.360 --> 00:34:24.200
In order the global market have any
sense of connection to farmers and

00:34:24.200 --> 00:34:26.790
actually the potential source
of great quality coffee.

00:34:26.790 --> 00:34:32.470
So he decided to form an organization
dedicated to making this connection and

00:34:32.470 --> 00:34:35.980
making the market work in favor of
small holder of coffee farmers.

00:34:37.910 --> 00:34:41.120
He looked at the global
operating environment.

00:34:41.120 --> 00:34:45.270
Recognized the market shocks,
low commodity prices, climate change and

00:34:45.270 --> 00:34:47.930
industry consolidation that
coffee farmers were facing.

00:34:49.100 --> 00:34:52.260
And he invented a new business model.

00:34:52.260 --> 00:34:56.160
He decided to go from a more traditional,
linear supply chain model,

00:34:56.160 --> 00:34:58.970
to a much more circular value chain.

00:34:58.970 --> 00:35:03.450
So he coined this relationship Coffee,
and the idea was that there would be full

00:35:03.450 --> 00:35:08.770
transparency across the value chain,
that all producers would have access

00:35:08.770 --> 00:35:13.120
to full information as would everyone
involved in every step of the value chain.

00:35:13.120 --> 00:35:16.695
So all prices and costs were
made transparent and shared, but

00:35:16.695 --> 00:35:21.096
more importantly, relationships were
built between those individuals so

00:35:21.096 --> 00:35:25.374
that sector wide issues could be
discussed and solutions could be found.

00:35:30.884 --> 00:35:34.621
He coined the purpose of
the organization as helping farmers

00:35:34.621 --> 00:35:38.180
move from subsistence to sustainability.

00:35:38.180 --> 00:35:42.340
So thinking about really taking
having an impact on some of

00:35:42.340 --> 00:35:45.860
the 25 million small holder coffee farmers
who are struggling to subsistence and

00:35:45.860 --> 00:35:49.250
moving them to a viable business,
a sustainable business model.

00:35:51.640 --> 00:35:56.060
One of the steps he did to reorient his
company, which is already quite strongly

00:35:56.060 --> 00:36:00.930
founded on social, economic, and
environmental vision, was to become a B

00:36:00.930 --> 00:36:06.400
corporation or a benefit corporation
in 2008 and hold the organization

00:36:06.400 --> 00:36:10.840
accountable to a series of metrics that
B corporations are accountable to.

00:36:10.840 --> 00:36:15.990
B corporations are approved to be,
and in fact required to be,

00:36:15.990 --> 00:36:19.620
if you get a B corporation status, to be
dedicated to social, environmental, and

00:36:19.620 --> 00:36:22.580
financial impact, and
then they are monitored accordingly.

00:36:22.580 --> 00:36:26.486
And sustainable harvest as you can see
on this grid performed quite well,

00:36:26.486 --> 00:36:28.629
even relative to other B Corporations.

00:36:32.264 --> 00:36:35.780
A second step he took was to
align culture and incentive.

00:36:35.780 --> 00:36:40.558
So to really think about all
the different aspects of compensation,

00:36:40.558 --> 00:36:44.234
hiring practices,

00:36:44.234 --> 00:36:48.760
transparency, and connection to farmers.

00:36:48.760 --> 00:36:53.424
So as one example, compensation on the
most highly compensated employee in his

00:36:53.424 --> 00:36:59.090
company is a ratio 5:1, to the lowest
compensated employee in the United States.

00:36:59.090 --> 00:36:59.930
Hiring practices,

00:36:59.930 --> 00:37:05.320
quite interesting he hired four systems
thinkers, not copy specialists.

00:37:05.320 --> 00:37:08.420
He looks to people who have shown
interest in other cultures,

00:37:08.420 --> 00:37:11.770
speak another language, and
think in an integrated way.

00:37:11.770 --> 00:37:14.627
Then he teaches them core skills
around the coffee industry.

00:37:20.705 --> 00:37:25.431
In terms of the learning orientation, one
of the primary ways sustainable harvest

00:37:25.431 --> 00:37:29.481
is developed this learning is through
an event that they've run now for

00:37:29.481 --> 00:37:32.270
several years, Let's Talk Coffee.

00:37:32.270 --> 00:37:36.420
It's an event that's held in most
usually a coffee growing country,

00:37:36.420 --> 00:37:39.890
and it brings together all of
the members of the value chain,

00:37:39.890 --> 00:37:44.000
to talk about upcoming issues, what
are the issues that they are not on top of

00:37:44.000 --> 00:37:46.800
that they anticipate coming down
the pipe for the coffee industry?

00:37:46.800 --> 00:37:48.670
What are producers struggling with?

00:37:48.670 --> 00:37:50.480
What are the needs of roasters?

00:37:50.480 --> 00:37:51.840
How do they problem solve together?

00:37:51.840 --> 00:37:54.970
And how do they anticipate
future challenges?

00:37:54.970 --> 00:37:58.300
In addition a lot of deals get
done at Let's Talk Coffee as

00:37:58.300 --> 00:38:03.200
roasters meet producers and get to know
their particular coffees and stories.

00:38:03.200 --> 00:38:06.957
But most importantly, it's
an opportunity to really problem solve,

00:38:06.957 --> 00:38:08.719
and continue to learn together.

00:38:11.597 --> 00:38:16.258
Innovation, one of the ways in which
sustainable harvest innovate it

00:38:16.258 --> 00:38:18.714
through new products and services.

00:38:18.714 --> 00:38:22.502
Case to on the left of the products
that they designed that's won several

00:38:22.502 --> 00:38:23.680
awards for innovation.

00:38:23.680 --> 00:38:29.620
That's really does for coffee what we have
for wine, which is a way of rating wines

00:38:29.620 --> 00:38:35.680
and sharing information between
producers and retailers of that product.

00:38:35.680 --> 00:38:39.095
Question coffee is a new band
that they developed to help

00:38:39.095 --> 00:38:46.280
farmers really work directly with buyers,
with customers.

00:38:46.280 --> 00:38:49.620
So linking farmers
directly to customers and

00:38:49.620 --> 00:38:52.170
helping them understand what the issues
are the farmers are facing and

00:38:52.170 --> 00:38:57.640
helping educate consumers about
questioning the cost of supply chain.

00:38:58.900 --> 00:39:02.580
And finally, they had a huge emphasis
on farmer training and technology.

00:39:02.580 --> 00:39:09.000
The company plows back 90% of the gross
revenues into farmer training and

00:39:09.000 --> 00:39:13.660
support including the prices paid for
the coffee purchased from farmers.

00:39:14.900 --> 00:39:20.655
Technology is included, water saving
technology for coffee processing.

00:39:20.655 --> 00:39:22.929
But also really high-tech strategies for

00:39:22.929 --> 00:39:26.540
thinking about how understand
the last mile of coffee production.

00:39:26.540 --> 00:39:29.702
Where you can look at
individual farmer plots and

00:39:29.702 --> 00:39:33.865
see how they connect to particular
coffees that are produced and

00:39:33.865 --> 00:39:39.202
understand the profile of the coffees
from a very microscale, individual plot.

00:39:41.958 --> 00:39:44.694
Through Let's Talk Coffee and
other platforms, and

00:39:44.694 --> 00:39:48.953
the deep relationships they've built
through their relationship coffee model,

00:39:48.953 --> 00:39:52.770
they've been able to engage broad
participation in problem-solving.

00:39:52.770 --> 00:39:57.872
So when the Roya Crisis broke
out in 2013 A fungus that was

00:39:57.872 --> 00:40:03.061
dramatically affecting coffee plants
in Central America in particular.

00:40:03.061 --> 00:40:07.729
Sustainable Harvest was positioned to
both perceive the problem very quickly

00:40:07.729 --> 00:40:11.687
because it has very regular visits
to its producers on the ground,

00:40:11.687 --> 00:40:13.400
through it origin offices.

00:40:13.400 --> 00:40:17.473
These offices based in countries of
origin where the coffee is grown.

00:40:17.473 --> 00:40:20.279
So they were able to perceive
the problem quickly and

00:40:20.279 --> 00:40:24.363
through their relationship leverage
scientists and agronomists to really

00:40:24.363 --> 00:40:28.728
figure out what were the best approaches
for averting the spread of the disease.

00:40:28.728 --> 00:40:32.507
Addressing plants that were already
diseased, educating farmers so

00:40:32.507 --> 00:40:35.325
that they would know
the tradeoff between different

00:40:35.325 --> 00:40:37.834
approaches like the use of fungicides or
not.

00:40:37.834 --> 00:40:42.079
And they convened several Let's Talk Roya
events where they brought different

00:40:42.079 --> 00:40:46.846
stakeholders together, provided training
kits to farmers, responded to questions.

00:40:46.846 --> 00:40:51.878
Even brought in lenders to help consider
how to assist the farmers with multi year

00:40:51.878 --> 00:40:56.619
loans for replanting a number of their
farms that had been so badly damaged.

00:41:01.136 --> 00:41:05.363
And then similar to Unilever working
creatively with diverse partnerships,

00:41:05.363 --> 00:41:08.480
Sustainable Harvest has done the same.

00:41:08.480 --> 00:41:12.680
In addition to working with
certifiers to validate and

00:41:12.680 --> 00:41:18.120
third party certify their fair trade and
ecologically, sustainably grown coffee.

00:41:18.120 --> 00:41:20.900
And Root Capital,
one of the lenders they work with.

00:41:20.900 --> 00:41:25.580
They formed a major partnership with
Bloomberg to use non-profit capital to

00:41:25.580 --> 00:41:31.120
reach tens of thousands of
Rwandan women who are displaced.

00:41:31.120 --> 00:41:34.140
And for whom coffee growing is
a wonderful opportunity for

00:41:34.140 --> 00:41:36.490
building a sustainable livelihood.

00:41:36.490 --> 00:41:40.362
So through these programs of training and
support,

00:41:40.362 --> 00:41:45.027
Rwandan women have been able to
not just cultivate coffee, but

00:41:45.027 --> 00:41:49.972
cultivate premium quality coffee and
reach consumers directly.

00:41:49.972 --> 00:41:53.672
So as we think about these examples and
what it means for

00:41:53.672 --> 00:41:58.420
building a sustainable organization,
we have lots of data in terms of

00:41:58.420 --> 00:42:03.904
the Harvard Business Review report that
summarizes recent academic research.

00:42:03.904 --> 00:42:08.336
And business experience related
to what the business case is for

00:42:08.336 --> 00:42:12.170
integrating sustainability at the core.

00:42:12.170 --> 00:42:15.232
And we've got global
moves by groups of CEOs

00:42:15.232 --> 00:42:19.877
to think about the UN Sustainable
Development Goals for example.

00:42:19.877 --> 00:42:23.583
The 17 goals that the United Nations
has put forward as our

00:42:23.583 --> 00:42:28.310
goals of a global community to
achieve sustainable development.

00:42:28.310 --> 00:42:33.247
And we now have CEOs articulating
the $12 trillion opportunity that

00:42:33.247 --> 00:42:34.815
those goals present.

00:42:34.815 --> 00:42:39.560
So what does this mean for your business?

00:42:39.560 --> 00:42:44.498
It's about reflecting on how your core
purpose might align with sustainability.

00:42:44.498 --> 00:42:48.701
And then thinking about
how to use orientations

00:42:48.701 --> 00:42:52.609
that will help drive you towards progress.

00:42:52.609 --> 00:42:56.580
Both in terms of achieving and
maximizing the impact of your purpose and

00:42:56.580 --> 00:43:00.720
thriving in the context of a highly
complex and changing environment.

00:43:00.720 --> 00:43:04.240
I think Paul Coleman sums
it up best when he says,

00:43:04.240 --> 00:43:06.960
we are at a turning point in history.

00:43:06.960 --> 00:43:08.510
A point where we all need to change for

00:43:08.510 --> 00:43:10.830
human life on the planet
to continue to prosper.

00:43:12.260 --> 00:43:15.650
A new business model with sustainability
at its heart is vital for

00:43:15.650 --> 00:43:18.750
quality of life around
the globe to improve.

00:43:18.750 --> 00:43:21.840
Only the businesses that
grasp this will survive.

00:43:21.840 --> 00:43:24.602
Only those who grow
sustainably with thrive.

00:43:24.602 --> 00:43:29.450
So I'm looking forward to your thoughts
and we can take a few questions.

00:43:29.450 --> 00:43:34.050
But first I'm going to turn it over to who
will give you a bit of information about

00:43:34.050 --> 00:43:35.320
our upcoming enrollment programme.

00:43:36.890 --> 00:43:39.500
&gt;&gt; Thank you very much Julia,
this was extremely interesting.

00:43:39.500 --> 00:43:43.510
And I think also inspiring to all of us
listening to the webinar to think about

00:43:43.510 --> 00:43:48.570
how we might be able to look differently
at the organizations that we work in and

00:43:48.570 --> 00:43:52.987
contribute to a more
sustainable society over time.

00:43:52.987 --> 00:43:56.970
So with that I'd like to
turn it over to Julia and

00:43:56.970 --> 00:43:59.430
we will engage in a few
questions that we'll be taking.

00:43:59.430 --> 00:44:03.140
Again we'll answer as many questions as
we can in the short time that we have.

00:44:03.140 --> 00:44:07.070
And a reminder that the recording of
the webinar will be sent to all of you

00:44:07.070 --> 00:44:11.010
who have been on the line with us today so
that they can review the material again.

00:44:11.010 --> 00:44:16.470
So Julia one question that has come
in has to do with the relationship,

00:44:16.470 --> 00:44:22.652
difference, and similarities
between corporate responsibilities,

00:44:22.652 --> 00:44:26.250
CSR and sustainability.

00:44:26.250 --> 00:44:28.870
And how you see that connection?

00:44:28.870 --> 00:44:29.820
&gt;&gt; Great question.

00:44:29.820 --> 00:44:33.690
And I think it is quite different
corporate sustainability

00:44:33.690 --> 00:44:36.098
of course there's a whole range and
spectrum.

00:44:36.098 --> 00:44:40.370
So, one mustn't over simplify that
corporate social responsibility

00:44:40.370 --> 00:44:44.210
can often not be linked to core
purpose of the organization.

00:44:44.210 --> 00:44:49.010
So it could be for example the donation
of products associated with that business

00:44:49.010 --> 00:44:49.970
to people who need them.

00:44:49.970 --> 00:44:52.090
Computers to classrooms or

00:44:52.090 --> 00:44:54.980
backpacks with particular products
to children in different schools.

00:44:56.990 --> 00:44:59.940
Sustainability at the core
is quite different.

00:44:59.940 --> 00:45:03.450
It means that you're basically putting
sustainability as core to your strategy.

00:45:04.830 --> 00:45:07.740
And designing your entire
business model and

00:45:07.740 --> 00:45:12.170
strategy around sustainability objectives.

00:45:12.170 --> 00:45:16.010
So really looking at how,
back to connecting with your environment,

00:45:16.010 --> 00:45:18.490
understanding it, building
relationships in your value chain.

00:45:18.490 --> 00:45:23.280
Understanding the social environmental
impact of your production processes and

00:45:23.280 --> 00:45:23.798
your product.

00:45:23.798 --> 00:45:30.270
Reorienting your whole system so that
it aligns with the goal sustainability.

00:45:30.270 --> 00:45:34.844
Which means taking significant
action internally to track different

00:45:34.844 --> 00:45:38.668
kinds of metrics and
that's obviously at one extreme.

00:45:38.668 --> 00:45:41.460
But then innovating of course for
sustainability where you're looking

00:45:41.460 --> 00:45:45.320
at new business models like the circular
economy or collaborative consumption.

00:45:46.850 --> 00:45:52.790
New ways of creating sustainable
sourcing and sustainable value chains so

00:45:52.790 --> 00:45:58.120
that it is fully permeated
throughout your organization.

00:45:58.120 --> 00:46:03.210
&gt;&gt; So you've given examples of two
kind of for-profit companies that have

00:46:03.210 --> 00:46:05.530
been tackling this challenge
of sustainability.

00:46:05.530 --> 00:46:07.840
What are some ways that
you've seen non-profit and

00:46:07.840 --> 00:46:10.760
government organizations engage
in these type of activity?

00:46:10.760 --> 00:46:15.480
&gt;&gt; Well, there's been a big
shift I would say in the last 20

00:46:15.480 --> 00:46:20.790
years to really sort of blur the lines
between the different sectors of society.

00:46:20.790 --> 00:46:24.840
I think we think of government as
providing some basic services,

00:46:24.840 --> 00:46:27.390
civil society being sort of
the watchdog of industry,

00:46:27.390 --> 00:46:29.870
and industry being there
to maximize profits.

00:46:29.870 --> 00:46:32.920
And I think what we're starting
to see is in the modern context,

00:46:32.920 --> 00:46:35.660
with the kinds of challenges that
we're facing in the 21st century,

00:46:36.780 --> 00:46:40.210
stakeholders are working kind
of around a new social compact.

00:46:40.210 --> 00:46:44.290
Where business, government, and civil
society need to find collaborative models

00:46:44.290 --> 00:46:47.650
for working together to
solve complex challenges.

00:46:47.650 --> 00:46:50.220
I think that the Tropical Forest Alliance
is a great example,

00:46:50.220 --> 00:46:54.820
where governments of countries that are
suffering from significant deforestation.

00:46:54.820 --> 00:46:59.131
Indonesia, for example, losing a lot
of tropical rainforest because of palm

00:46:59.131 --> 00:47:03.460
oil production has a lot at
stake in thinking, you know,

00:47:03.460 --> 00:47:06.890
getting businesses who are driving that
deforestation to think differently.

00:47:06.890 --> 00:47:10.690
And then the businesses who are
competitors who are growing palm oil have

00:47:10.690 --> 00:47:12.160
a lot at stake in working together.

00:47:12.160 --> 00:47:13.470
Because if one business says okay,

00:47:13.470 --> 00:47:19.000
we'll take deforestation out of our supply
chain, but the other competitors don't,

00:47:19.000 --> 00:47:22.190
they will certainly lose competitive
advantage, at least in the short term.

00:47:22.190 --> 00:47:27.240
So this collaboration between competitors
within business, engaging governments, and

00:47:27.240 --> 00:47:31.530
then also civil society organizations
who often have a lot of scientific and

00:47:31.530 --> 00:47:36.560
community knowledge in terms of how
to make change are really essential.

00:47:38.060 --> 00:47:42.395
&gt;&gt; Great, so we've had a number of
questions come up around metrics and

00:47:42.395 --> 00:47:43.830
measuring success.

00:47:43.830 --> 00:47:49.060
I think you've shared a number of
examples from Unilever on how they

00:47:49.060 --> 00:47:53.760
measure both their financial impact
as well as their sustainability.

00:47:53.760 --> 00:47:55.700
Or environmental impact.

00:47:55.700 --> 00:48:01.010
Do you have other examples from
other companies or what should

00:48:01.010 --> 00:48:04.410
somebody think about as they're designing
some metrics for their organization?

00:48:04.410 --> 00:48:09.612
&gt;&gt; Well, I think first is having a
thorough understanding of what the impacts

00:48:09.612 --> 00:48:14.663
are and of course, having a clear
understanding of what your goals are.

00:48:14.663 --> 00:48:17.850
And then designing custom
metrics accordingly.

00:48:17.850 --> 00:48:20.040
But certainly,
the templates that are out there,

00:48:20.040 --> 00:48:22.460
I think the B corporation
template is a great template.

00:48:22.460 --> 00:48:25.872
It looks at all the environmental,
social, and governance factors.

00:48:25.872 --> 00:48:30.224
So human rights is becoming a stronger and
stronger issue now,

00:48:30.224 --> 00:48:34.425
and most certifications don't
actually address that yet.

00:48:34.425 --> 00:48:38.310
So businesses that are kind of at
the forefront, back to kind of perceiving

00:48:38.310 --> 00:48:42.250
changes in the environment and
anticipating and responding.

00:48:42.250 --> 00:48:44.540
They are perceiving that human
rights is becoming a bigger and

00:48:44.540 --> 00:48:48.320
bigger issue, slave labor and
other labor rights issues.

00:48:48.320 --> 00:48:51.810
And so they are already proactively
tackling those even though the full

00:48:51.810 --> 00:48:55.000
systems and metrics and
measurement tools are not yet in place.

00:48:55.000 --> 00:48:58.540
Sustainable Harvest, for example,
developed its own system where it uses its

00:48:58.540 --> 00:49:03.850
staff at the origin offices to go and
conduct surveys of producer

00:49:03.850 --> 00:49:08.416
groups to identify whether or not there
are any issues related to slave labor.

00:49:08.416 --> 00:49:12.099
It's not yet third-party certified,
but it's at least one indicator to

00:49:12.099 --> 00:49:15.190
Sustainable Harvest if there
are problems and issues there.

00:49:15.190 --> 00:49:18.117
So, it's part looking at
the models that are out there and

00:49:18.117 --> 00:49:22.305
then it's part anticipating and perceiving
what are becoming current concern and

00:49:22.305 --> 00:49:25.320
then designing your own tool
that suite your own industry.

00:49:27.210 --> 00:49:29.070
&gt;&gt; Great.
Another question that came up

00:49:29.070 --> 00:49:30.720
had to do with geography.

00:49:30.720 --> 00:49:34.540
So are there particular geographies
where you see more energy and

00:49:34.540 --> 00:49:38.770
activity around this or
where this is perhaps more required?

00:49:38.770 --> 00:49:44.270
Or is this more of
a global level activity?

00:49:44.270 --> 00:49:46.430
&gt;&gt; That's a very good question.

00:49:46.430 --> 00:49:49.668
I mean, of course there are different
drivers in different regions.

00:49:49.668 --> 00:49:55.071
But if you look at the developing world,
economic term called emerging markets,

00:49:55.071 --> 00:49:58.380
I like to consider more
of developing countries.

00:49:58.380 --> 00:50:02.890
There are a lot of challenges that
are being faced there in terms of resource

00:50:02.890 --> 00:50:07.640
scarcity, moves from rural
to urban environment, and

00:50:07.640 --> 00:50:10.900
the stress that places on
already strained infrastructure.

00:50:10.900 --> 00:50:13.552
And so there are tremendous
opportunities for innovation there.

00:50:13.552 --> 00:50:18.400
And one thing Unilever has recognized,
and other major companies has recognized,

00:50:18.400 --> 00:50:22.290
that if they don't use their basis
in these countries to innovate in

00:50:22.290 --> 00:50:24.837
response to these incredible challenges.

00:50:24.837 --> 00:50:27.604
There are plenty of local
competitors emerging who

00:50:27.604 --> 00:50:30.186
are taking on those
challenges in innovating.

00:50:30.186 --> 00:50:33.137
So, in a sense they have
to re-orient that way and

00:50:33.137 --> 00:50:37.940
build their local capacity to innovate
in response to some of these challenges.

00:50:38.980 --> 00:50:43.378
In terms of sustainability demand from
consumers, there's certainly a lot

00:50:43.378 --> 00:50:47.573
more in the United States than Europe
that's driving a lot of the quest for

00:50:47.573 --> 00:50:50.434
product that could be
certified as sustainable.

00:50:50.434 --> 00:50:55.012
Though, it really depends where you look,
but again, taking assistance perfecting,

00:50:55.012 --> 00:50:56.840
these are global challenges.

00:50:56.840 --> 00:51:01.393
And the key is to then orient based on
where you have an impact to align with

00:51:01.393 --> 00:51:04.169
the sustainability challenges and goals.

00:51:04.169 --> 00:51:07.630
&gt;&gt; All right, so we are,
thank you very much Julia.

00:51:07.630 --> 00:51:13.139
We're at the top of the hour so we do
not have time for additional questions.

00:51:13.139 --> 00:51:16.020
But hopefully,
you all have enjoyed the webinar and

00:51:16.020 --> 00:51:19.860
the information that has been
presented as much as I have.

00:51:19.860 --> 00:51:22.805
I'd like to encourage all of
